.\" Hand-written man(7) fixture: one page exercising the macro set.
.TH GREET 1 "August 2026" "greet 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
greet, hello \- write a greeting to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B greet
[\fB\-n\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fIFILE\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Write
.I "a friendly greeting"
to standard output, one per
.IR FILE .
With no FILE, or when FILE is \-, read standard input.
.PP
The greeting is written in the current locale, and a trailing
newline is always added\(em even in quiet mode.
.SS Exit behaviour
Errors are reported on standard error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name =\fINAME\fR
Greet \fINAME\fR instead of the world.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress the greeting, printing only the exit status.
.RS
Useful in scripts.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display this help and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
greet \-n Ada
greet \-\-quiet < names.txt
.EE
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 on success, and 1 when a FILE cannot be read.
.SH NOTES
Three shell metacharacters are special:
.IP \(bu 2
the pipe,
.IP \(bu 2
the ampersand,
.IP \(bu 2
the semicolon.
.PP
See
.UR https://example.org/greet
the project page
.UE
for details.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR echo (1),
.BR printf (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Written by A. Coder.
